[2m[2mSession[0m: [1;33mdeepseek_deepseek-v4-flash-integration-bug-trial-1[0m[0m
Fixed. All three invariants hold across 25 runs of `main.py` and a 50-run stress test (varied quantities 1–3, 20 concurrent orders, 40% failure rate).

## Root cause

`checkout.py` did **check-then-act across awaits**: `check_stock` (line 13) and `decrement` (line 23) are separate async operations with the payment in between, so every concurrent order saw `stock >= quantity` and most got charged, but only 5 decrements could win. The losers were charged with no item — the ghost charges in the reproduction ($800–$1000 charged, $500 expected). The same split check/act is what lets inventory go negative under other interleavings.

## Changes

- **`checkout.py`** — reordered to reserve → charge → release. Stock is reserved atomically *before* any payment; a failed payment releases the reservation. The "inventory error after payment" path is gone because it's now impossible to charge without holding stock.
- **`inventory.py:19`** — added `Inventory.reserve(quantity)`: availability check and decrement in one synchronous block (no `await` between them), so concurrent checkouts can't oversell.
- **`payments.py:21`** — added `PaymentGateway.charge_once(order_id, amount)`: idempotent per order — a retry for an already-charged order returns success without a second charge.

Public interfaces of `Inventory` and `PaymentGateway` are unchanged; both additions are new methods. Releasing uses the existing `increment`.

Verified per-run: stock never below zero, `total_charged == successful × price`, zero duplicate charges, and the set of charged order IDs exactly equals the set of successful orders.
